E17 — response-file expansion on the native backend
----------------------------------------------------
  file named `inject` containing `-version`:
    konanc @inject         -> exit 0, stdout "Kotlin/Native: 2.4.10"   EXPANDED
    konanc @./inject       -> exit 0, stdout "Kotlin/Native: 2.4.10"   EXPANDED
    konanc @/abs/inject    -> exit 0, stdout "Kotlin/Native: 2.4.10"   EXPANDED
  file named `@inject` containing `-version`:
    konanc @inject         -> warning: argfile not found: .../inject
                              error: you have not specified any compilation
                              arguments.  exit 1
    konanc /abs/@inject    -> error: source entry is not a Kotlin file  exit 1
    konanc ./@inject       -> error: source entry is not a Kotlin file  exit 1
    konanc @nonexistent    -> warning: argfile not found ... (warning only)
Corrected rule: expansion is decided by the FIRST CHARACTER OF THE ARGV TOKEN,
not by whether the path is absolute. The `@` is stripped and the remainder is
the response-file path, absolute or not. A driver that passes package paths as
absolute paths starting with `/` therefore never produces an expandable token —
which is why the absolute-path discipline is a sound structural backstop, and
why the reason for it must be stated as the token's first character.

